---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site';
const title = `How We Calculate | ${SITE.name}`;
const description = 'The formulas, assumptions and data sources behind every calculator on this site.';
const canonical = '/methodology';
---
<BaseLayout {title} {description} {canonical}>
  <div class="page-band">
    <div class="page">
      <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/">Home</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><span aria-current="page">How we calculate</span>
      </nav>
      <div class="tool-hero"><h1>How we calculate</h1>
      <p>Every formula, every default, and everything each model deliberately leaves out.</p>
    </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="page">
    <section class="prose">
      <h2>Core formulas</h2>
      <p>All payment figures come from the standard level-payment annuity formula:</p>
      <div class="formula">M = P × [ r(1 + r)ⁿ ] / [ (1 + r)ⁿ − 1 ]</div>
      <p>
        where P is principal, r is the monthly rate (annual ÷ 12) and n is the number
        of payments. Amortization schedules are generated iteratively rather than
        from a closed form, because extra principal payments alter the balance path
        and therefore every subsequent interest charge.
      </p>
      <p>
        Where a tool compares two options, it compares <strong>net position</strong>:
        total cash paid out plus debt still outstanding at each point in time.
        Comparing monthly payments alone silently rewards whichever option has the
        longer term, which is why a reset 30-year refinance looks better than it is.
      </p>

      <h2>Defaults</h2>
      <p>
        Defaults are illustrative starting points, not forecasts or recommendations.
        Rates, prices and growth assumptions shipped with each calculator are chosen
        to be plausible rather than current. Replace every one of them with your own
        figures — the defaults exist so the page renders something meaningful before
        you type, not because we think they apply to you.
      </p>

      <h2>Data sources</h2>
      <p>
        State-level transfer taxes, recording fees and attorney requirements are
        compiled from state departments of revenue and published closing-custom
        tables. Property tax rates derive from Census ACS median tax and median value
        figures. Insurance averages come from NAIC reporting. Metro prices, incomes
        and rents come from Census ACS tables.
      </p>
      <div class="note note--warn">
        <strong class="note__title">Verification status</strong>
        The state and metro data sets currently ship as seeded estimates and are
        marked as unverified on every page that uses them. They have not yet been
        checked against primary sources. Do not rely on them for a transaction.
      </div>

      <h2>What the models exclude</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Taxes.</strong> All figures are pre-tax. Mortgage interest deductibility, depreciation on rentals, capital gains treatment and depreciation recapture are excluded, because each depends on circumstances a calculator cannot know.</li>
        <li><strong>Rate variability.</strong> Fixed rates are held constant; variable rates (HELOCs, ARMs) are modelled at the rate you enter and do not simulate movement.</li>
        <li><strong>Lender-specific pricing.</strong> Points, credits, and loan-level price adjustments vary by lender and credit profile.</li>
        <li><strong>Sequence risk.</strong> Investment returns are applied as a smooth annual rate. Real returns arrive unevenly, and the order matters.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Rounding</h2>
      <p>
        Calculations run at full floating-point precision and are rounded only for
        display. Totals may therefore differ by a cent or two from the sum of the
        displayed rows. Final payments in an amortization schedule are approximate,
        as servicers apply their own day-count and rounding conventions.
      </p>

      <h2>Corrections</h2>
      <p>
        If a figure here disagrees with a source you trust, the source is more likely
        to be right. These tools are estimates for orientation, not statements of
        fact about your transaction.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
